OAI_PAPER({"slug": "An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026", "title": "An isomorphism of the free group factors", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026.pdf", "family": "287", "family_title": "Isomorphism of the free group factors", "abstract_html": "We solve the free group factor isomorphism problem affirmatively by proving that \\(L(\\mathbb F_2)\\) and \\(L(\\mathbb F_3)\\) are isomorphic as tracial von Neumann algebras. The classical free group factor alternative then implies that all interpolated free group factors, including \\(L(\\mathbb F_\\infty)\\), are isomorphic and have fundamental group \\(\\mathbb R_{\\gt 0}\\).", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1 (Isomorphism of consecutive ranks)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a unital trace-preserving normal \\(*\\)-isomorphism \\( L(\\mathbb F_n)\\cong L(\\mathbb F_{n+1}). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a discrete group \\(G\\), let\n  \\[\n  L(G)=\\{\\lambda(g):g\\in G\\}'',\n  \\qquad\n  (\\lambda(g)\\xi)(h)=\\xi(g^{-1}h),\n  \\]\n  with canonical trace \\(\\tau(x)=\\langle x\\delta_e,\\delta_e\\rangle\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbb F_d\\) denote the free group on \\(d\\) generators.</p><p class=\"li\">A normal map between von Neumann algebras is an ultraweakly continuous map.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq 3\\) be an integer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a unital trace-preserving normal \\(*\\)-isomorphism\n\\[\nL(\\mathbb F_n)\\cong L(\\mathbb F_{n+1}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(n\\geq3\\) and put \\(M=L(\\mathbb F_{n+1})\\), with its canonical trace and canonical freely generating Haar tuple\n\\[\n(A_1^{(0)},\\ldots,A_n^{(0)},C^{(0)}).\n\\]\nWrite \\(A^{(k)}=(A_1^{(k)},\\ldots,A_n^{(k)})\\).</p><p>First record the continuity estimate used below. For unitary \\(d\\)-tuples \\(U,V\\), put\n\\[\nd_2(U,V)=\\max_{1\\leq j\\leq d}\\|U_j-V_j\\|_2.\n\\]\nIf \\(v\\) is a group word of length \\(|v|\\), telescoping gives\n\\[\n\\|U_v-V_v\\|_2\\leq |v|\\,d_2(U,V).\n\\]\nMultiplication on either side by a unitary preserves \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_2\\), and\n\\[\n\\|U_j^*-V_j^*\\|_2=\\|U_j-V_j\\|_2.\n\\]\nThus a finite word polynomial \\(F=\\sum_{\\nu=1}^qc_\\nu v_\\nu\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|F(U)-F(V)\\|_2\\leq L(F)d_2(U,V),\n\\qquad\nL(F)=\\sum_{\\nu=1}^q|c_\\nu|\\,|v_\\nu|.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nThe same telescoping argument gives operator-norm continuity.</p><p>Choose a sequence \\((y_j)_{j\\geq1}\\), dense in \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\), consisting of elements of the rational complex group word algebra of the initial tuple. Such a sequence exists by Proposition 1, and coefficients in \\(\\mathbb Q+i\\mathbb Q\\) make this algebra countable. Put \\(r_0=1\\).</p><p>Construct freely generating Haar tuples \\((A^{(k)},C^{(k)})\\) and positive numbers \\(r_k\\) inductively. At stage \\(k\\geq1\\), make the choices in the following order.</p><p>First, for each \\(1\\leq j\\leq k\\), choose a finite linear combination \\(F_{j,k}\\) of group words in \\(n+1\\) variables with\n\\[\n\\|F_{j,k}(A^{(k-1)},C^{(k-1)})-y_j\\|_2&lt;2^{-k}.\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nThis is possible because the current completed tuple generates \\(M\\), and its word algebra is dense in \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\). Let \\(H_k\\geq0\\) bound the finitely many constants \\(L(F_{j,k})\\). Choose\n\\[\n0&lt;\\epsilon_k&lt;\n\\min\\left\\{\\frac{r_{k-1}}2,\\frac{2^{-k}}{1+H_k}\\right\\}.\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]</p><p>Next apply Proposition 5 to \\((A^{(k-1)},C^{(k-1)})\\), with tolerance \\(\\epsilon_k\\). Let \\(\\alpha_k,w_k\\) be the resulting automorphism and word, and put\n\\[\nA_j^{(k)}=\\alpha_k(A_j^{(k-1)}),\n\\qquad\nC^{(k)}=\\alpha_k(C^{(k-1)}).\n\\]\nThe completed tuple remains freely generating Haar, and\n\\[\n\\max_j\\|A_j^{(k)}-A_j^{(k-1)}\\|&lt;\\epsilon_k,\n\\qquad\n\\|A_{w_k}^{(k)}-C^{(k-1)}\\|&lt;\\epsilon_k.\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nThe last expression approximates the old completion, as required in the preceding approximations.</p><p>Now define polynomials in \\(n\\) variables by\n\\[\nG_{j,k}(X)=F_{j,k}(X,X_{w_k})\n\\qquad(1\\leq j\\leq k).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\|x\\|_2\\leq\\|x\\|\\), the continuity estimate gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|G_{j,k}(A^{(k)})-y_j\\|_2\n&amp;\\leq\n\\|F_{j,k}(A^{(k)},A_{w_k}^{(k)})\n-F_{j,k}(A^{(k-1)},C^{(k-1)})\\|_2\\\\\n&amp;\\quad+\n\\|F_{j,k}(A^{(k-1)},C^{(k-1)})-y_j\\|_2\\\\\n&amp;&lt;H_k\\epsilon_k+2^{-k}\n&lt;2\\cdot2^{-k}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(5.6)\n\\]\nThis estimate treats \\(A_{w_k}^{(k)}\\) as a single unitary slot of \\(F_{j,k}\\). Thus the tolerance was chosen without depending on the length of the subsequently produced word.</p><p>Finally, let \\(L_k\\geq0\\) be a common \\(L^2\\)-Lipschitz bound for the finitely many \\(G_{j,k}\\), and choose\n\\[\n0&lt;r_k\\leq\n\\min\\left\\{\\frac{r_{k-1}}2,\\frac{2^{-k}}{1+L_k}\\right\\}.\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nThe constants are finite by the continuity estimate. They are chosen after \\(w_k\\) is known, so any increase in word length is accommodated in the budget for future steps. Both quantities in the minimum are positive.</p><p>For \\(\\ell&gt;k\\), geometric decrease gives\n\\[\n\\epsilon_\\ell&lt;\\frac{r_{\\ell-1}}2\n\\leq\\frac{r_k}{2^{\\ell-k}}.\n\\]\nHence, for \\(p&gt;k\\),\n\\[\n\\max_j\\|A_j^{(p)}-A_j^{(k)}\\|\n\\leq\\sum_{\\ell=k+1}^p\\epsilon_\\ell\n\\leq r_k.\n\\]\nSince \\(r_k\\leq2^{-k}r_0\\), each coordinate converges in operator norm to a unitary \\(A_j^{(\\infty)}\\), and\n\\[\n\\max_j\\|A_j^{(\\infty)}-A_j^{(k)}\\|\\leq r_k.\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nThe unitary group is norm closed. For every nonidentity reduced \\(v\\in\\mathbb F_n\\), continuity of word evaluation and the trace gives\n\\[\n\\tau(A_v^{(\\infty)})\n=\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\\tau(A_v^{(k)})=0.\n\\]\nThus the limiting tuple has the free Haar word distribution.</p><p>It remains to prove generation. For \\(j\\leq k\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|G_{j,k}(A^{(\\infty)})-y_j\\|_2\n&amp;\\leq\n\\|G_{j,k}(A^{(\\infty)})-G_{j,k}(A^{(k)})\\|_2\\\\\n&amp;\\quad+\\|G_{j,k}(A^{(k)})-y_j\\|_2\\\\\n&amp;\\leq L_kr_k+2\\cdot2^{-k}\n\\leq3\\cdot2^{-k}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(5.9)\n\\]\nFixing \\(j\\) and letting \\(k\\to\\infty\\) through \\(k\\geq j\\) puts \\(y_j\\) in the \\(L^2\\) closure of the word algebra of \\(A^{(\\infty)}\\). Since the \\(y_j\\) are dense, that word algebra is dense in \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nN=W^*(A_1^{(\\infty)},\\ldots,A_n^{(\\infty)}).\n\\]\nThe trace-preserving conditional expectation \\(E_N:M\\to N\\) extends to the orthogonal projection of \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\) onto \\(L^2(N,\\tau)\\). It is \\(L^2\\)-contractive and fixes every element of \\(N\\). Since \\(G_{j,k}(A^{(\\infty)})\\in N\\),\n\\[\n\\|E_N(y_j)-y_j\\|_2\n\\leq2\\|G_{j,k}(A^{(\\infty)})-y_j\\|_2\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThus the projection fixes a dense family and is the identity on \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\). For \\(x\\in M\\), faithfulness implies \\(E_N(x)=x\\), so \\(N=M\\).</p><p>The limiting tuple is a freely generating Haar \\(n\\)-tuple in \\(M\\). Proposition 1 gives a unital trace-preserving normal \\(*\\)-isomorphism\n\\[\nL(\\mathbb F_n)\\longrightarrow M=L(\\mathbb F_{n+1}).\n\\]</p><p>As recorded in original Remark 5.2, only the first \\(n\\) generators are required to converge in operator norm. The completions \\(C^{(k)}\\) may vary without a limit. The target approximations are in \\(L^2\\), and the conditional-expectation argument permits the operator norms of the approximating polynomials to grow with \\(k\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exists a unital normal trace-preserving \\(*\\)-isomorphism \\( \\Phi:L(\\mathbb F_2)\\longrightarrow L(\\mathbb F_3). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The free group factors carry their canonical normalized traces.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists a unital normal trace-preserving \\(*\\)-isomorphism\n\\[\n\\Phi:L(\\mathbb F_2)\\longrightarrow L(\\mathbb F_3).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Recall the amplification convention from Theorem 3’s definitions. Projections of every positive finite trace exist in the stabilized factor, and equivalence of projections of equal finite trace makes the normalized corner independent of the projection chosen.</p><p>Amplification respects normal tracial isomorphisms. If \\(\\Theta:Q\\to R\\) is such an isomorphism, then\n\\[\n\\Theta\\otimes\\operatorname{id}\n\\]\nis normal, preserves the semifinite trace, and sends a projection \\(p\\) of trace \\(t\\) to a projection \\(p'\\) of the same trace. Its restriction is an onto normal isomorphism between the corners, sends their units \\(p\\) and \\(p'\\) to one another, and preserves the traces normalized by \\(t^{-1}\\). Denote it by\n\\[\n\\Theta^t:Q^t\\to R^t.\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(N_s=L(\\mathbb F_s)\\) for real \\(s&gt;1\\). The established amplification formula is\n\\[\nN_s^t\\cong N_{1+(s-1)t^{-2}}\n\\qquad(s&gt;1,\\ t&gt;0).\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nThis is reference [3, Theorem 2.4] in the paper. It is unconditional and does not assume that distinct parameters give distinct isomorphism classes; it is the only interpolation theorem used here.</p><p>Apply Theorem 1 at \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\):\n\\[\nN_3\\cong N_4\\cong N_5.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\Theta:N_3\\to N_5\\) be the composition. Amplifying by \\(\\sqrt2\\) gives a unital normal trace-preserving isomorphism\n\\[\n\\Theta^{\\sqrt2}:N_3^{\\sqrt2}\\longrightarrow N_5^{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\nThe amplification formula identifies\n\\[\nN_3^{\\sqrt2}\n\\cong N_{1+(3-1)/2}=N_2,\n\\qquad\nN_5^{\\sqrt2}\n\\cong N_{1+(5-1)/2}=N_3.\n\\]\nThese identifications and the amplified map compose to give\n\\[\n\\Phi:L(\\mathbb F_2)\\longrightarrow L(\\mathbb F_3).\n\\]\nEvery map in the composition is a unital normal trace-preserving \\(*\\)-isomorphism. Hence \\(\\Phi\\) is bijective and has all the asserted properties.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 6.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For all \\(1<r,s\\leq\\infty\\), there is a unital normal trace-preserving \\(*\\)-isomorphism \\( L(\\mathbb F_r)\\cong L(\\mathbb F_s). \\) Moreover, \\( \\mathcal F(L(\\mathbb F_r))=\\mathbb R_{>0} \\qquad(1<r\\leq\\infty). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For \\(1&lt;r&lt;\\infty\\), let \\(L(\\mathbb F_r)\\) be the interpolated free group factor, agreeing with the usual group factor at integer parameters. Put\n  \\[\n  L(\\mathbb F_\\infty)=L(\\text{the countably generated free group}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor \\((Q,\\tau_Q)\\) and \\(t&gt;0\\), choose a projection\n  \\(p\\in Q\\overline\\otimes B(\\ell^2)\\) with\n  \\((\\tau_Q\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr})(p)=t\\), and define\n  \\[\n  Q^t=p(Q\\overline\\otimes B(\\ell^2))p,\n  \\qquad\n  \\tau_{Q^t}=t^{-1}(\\tau_Q\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr})|_{Q^t}.\n  \\]\n  Here \\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\) gives each rank-one projection trace one. The normalized corner is independent of the choice of \\(p\\), up to normal trace-preserving \\(*\\)-isomorphism.</p><p class=\"li\">The fundamental group is\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F(Q)=\\{t&gt;0:Q^t\\cong Q\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all \\(1&lt;r,s\\leq\\infty\\), there is a unital normal trace-preserving \\(*\\)-isomorphism\n\\[\nL(\\mathbb F_r)\\cong L(\\mathbb F_s).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\mathcal F(L(\\mathbb F_r))=\\mathbb R_{&gt;0}\n\\qquad(1&lt;r\\leq\\infty).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(N_r=L(\\mathbb F_r)\\), including \\(N_\\infty=L(\\mathbb F_\\infty)\\).</p><p>The classical free group factor alternative says that either all \\(N_r\\), \\(1&lt;r\\leq\\infty\\), are isomorphic, or\n\\[\nN_r\\not\\cong N_s\n\\qquad(1&lt;r&lt;s\\leq\\infty).\n\\]\nThe inclusion of the infinite parameter is Rădulescu’s dichotomy, reference [11, Corollary 4.7], restated in [5, Theorem 1.1]. Dykema’s corresponding dichotomy, [3, Corollary 4.2], is stated for finite parameters.</p><p>Theorem 2 rules out the pairwise-nonisomorphic alternative. Thus all parameters, including \\(\\infty\\), give the same isomorphism class.</p><p>For finite \\(r&gt;1\\) and every \\(t&gt;0\\), the amplification formula used in Theorem 2 gives\n\\[\nN_r^t\\cong N_{1+(r-1)t^{-2}}\\cong N_r.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(\\mathcal F(N_r)=\\mathbb R_{&gt;0}\\).</p><p>Finally, \\(N_\\infty\\cong N_2\\), and functoriality of amplification gives\n\\[\nN_\\infty^t\\cong N_2^t\\cong N_2\\cong N_\\infty\n\\qquad(t&gt;0).\n\\]\nHence \\(\\mathcal F(N_\\infty)=\\mathbb R_{&gt;0}\\) as well.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary 7.1 (Dependence on von Neumann generators)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every integer \\(n\\geq2\\), there are a self-adjoint \\(n\\)-tuple \\(X^{(n)}\\) and a self-adjoint \\(2n\\)-tuple \\(Y^{(n)}\\) such that \\( W^*(X^{(n)})=W^*(Y^{(n)})=M \\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M=L(\\mathbb F_2)\\), with its canonical trace.</p><p class=\"li\">For a bounded self-adjoint tuple \\(Z\\), let \\(\\delta(Z)\\) and \\(\\delta_0(Z)\\) denote Voiculescu’s microstates free entropy dimensions, the latter being the modified version using entropy in the presence of the perturbing semicircular family.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\delta^*(Z)\\) and \\(\\delta^\\star(Z)\\) denote the nonmicrostates dimensions defined through nonmicrostates free entropy \\(\\chi^*\\) and free Fisher information \\(\\Phi^*\\), respectively, with the conventions of references [17, Section 2.4] and [2, Section 4.1.1] in the paper.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(n\\geq2\\), there are a self-adjoint \\(n\\)-tuple \\(X^{(n)}\\) and a self-adjoint \\(2n\\)-tuple \\(Y^{(n)}\\) such that\n\\[\nW^*(X^{(n)})=W^*(Y^{(n)})=M\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\delta(X^{(n)})=\\delta_0(X^{(n)})=n,\n\\qquad\n\\delta^*(Y^{(n)})=\\delta^\\star(Y^{(n)})=n.\n\\]\nConsequently, none of \\(\\delta,\\delta_0,\\delta^*,\\delta^\\star\\) is invariant under changing a finite self-adjoint \\(W^*\\)-generating tuple of a tracial von Neumann algebra. Each takes unbounded values on such generating tuples of the single factor \\(L(\\mathbb F_2)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(n\\geq2\\). Theorem 2 and successive applications of Theorem 1 give a unital normal trace-preserving \\(*\\)-isomorphism\n\\[\n\\theta_n:L(\\mathbb F_n)\\longrightarrow M.\n\\]\nFor \\(n=2\\), take the identity.</p><p>For every finite self-adjoint tuple \\(Z\\) in \\(L(\\mathbb F_n)\\) and noncommutative \\(*\\)-polynomial \\(p\\),\n\\[\n\\tau_M\\bigl(p(\\theta_n(Z))\\bigr)\n=\\tau_{L(\\mathbb F_n)}(p(Z)).\n\\]\nCoordinatewise transport therefore preserves the joint tracial \\(*\\)-distribution. By their definitions, all four dimensions depend only on this distribution, so transport preserves each of them. Normality and surjectivity also give\n\\[\nW^*(\\theta_n(Z))=\\theta_n(W^*(Z)).\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\nS^{(n)}=(s_1,\\ldots,s_n)\n\\]\nbe a freely independent variance-one semicircular tuple in the semicircular realization of \\(L(\\mathbb F_n)\\), as in reference [15, Remarks 1.10–1.11]. It generates \\(L(\\mathbb F_n)\\) as a von Neumann algebra, and the standard semicircular computations give\n\\[\n\\delta(S^{(n)})=\\delta_0(S^{(n)})=n.\n\\]\nThe paper cites [17, Section 2.4(c),(d)] for \\(\\delta\\) and [1, Section 5, opening paragraph] for \\(\\delta_0\\). Define\n\\[\nX^{(n)}=\\theta_n(S^{(n)}).\n\\]\nTransport gives the required generation and microstates values.</p><p>For the nonmicrostates dimensions, let \\(u_1,\\ldots,u_n\\) be the canonical group unitaries in \\(L(\\mathbb F_n)\\), and put\n\\[\na_j=\\frac{u_j+u_j^*}{2},\n\\qquad\nb_j=\\frac{u_j-u_j^*}{2i},\n\\qquad\nG^{(n)}=(a_1,b_1,\\ldots,a_n,b_n).\n\\]\nThe entries are self-adjoint, and\n\\[\nu_j=a_j+ib_j,\n\\qquad\nu_j^*=a_j-ib_j.\n\\]\nThus they generate \\(\\mathbb C\\mathbb F_n\\) as a unital \\(*\\)-algebra and \\(L(\\mathbb F_n)\\) as a von Neumann algebra.</p><p>Apply Mineyev and Shlyakhtenko’s group-algebra theorem, reference [8, Theorem 4.1], before transporting this tuple. It gives\n\\[\n\\delta^*(G^{(n)})\n=\\delta^\\star(G^{(n)})\n=\\beta_1^{(2)}(\\mathbb F_n)-\\beta_0^{(2)}(\\mathbb F_n)+1\n=n.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\beta_0^{(2)}(\\mathbb F_n)=0,\n\\qquad\n\\beta_1^{(2)}(\\mathbb F_n)=n-1,\n\\]\nthe usual \\(L^2\\)-Betti numbers of the free group, as in [2, Section 2.1.1].</p><p>Define\n\\[\nY^{(n)}=\\theta_n(G^{(n)}).\n\\]\nIts joint tracial \\(*\\)-distribution is unchanged and it generates \\(M\\), proving the remaining assertions. Taking \\(n=2\\) and \\(n=3\\) gives different values on generating tuples of the same factor, and letting \\(n\\) grow gives unboundedness.</p><p>The nonmicrostates computation uses algebraic generators of \\(\\mathbb C\\mathbb F_n\\) before transport. The transported tuples all generate \\(L(\\mathbb F_2)\\) as a von Neumann algebra, but their algebraic \\(*\\)-algebras are not held fixed as \\(n\\) varies. The conclusion concerns \\(W^*\\)-generation and is compatible with the algebraic-generator invariance in [8, Theorem 4.1]. The microstates and nonmicrostates tuple families need not coincide.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Definition 2.1, Lemma 2.2 (Identification by a generating Haar tuple), and the accompanying tracial preliminaries", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a freely generating Haar \\(d\\)-tuple \\(U\\) in \\(M\\), the assignment \\( \\lambda(x_j)\\longmapsto U_j \\) extends to a unital normal trace-preserving \\(*\\)-isomorphism \\( L(\\mathbb F_d)\\longrightarrow M. \\) In …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((M,\\tau)\\) be a von Neumann algebra with faithful normal tracial state.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\|x\\|_2=\\tau(x^*x)^{1/2},\n  \\qquad\n  \\|x\\|=\\text{operator norm of }x.\n  \\]\n  The Hilbert space completion is \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\), with inner product\n  \\[\n  \\langle x,y\\rangle=\\tau(y^*x),\n  \\]\n  linear in the first variable. Left multiplication is the faithful normal representation of \\(M\\) on this space.</p><p class=\"li\">A family \\((B_i)_{i\\in I}\\) of unital subalgebras is free if\n  \\[\n  \\tau(b_1\\cdots b_r)=0\n  \\]\n  whenever \\(r\\geq1\\), \\(b_j\\in B_{i_j}\\), \\(\\tau(b_j)=0\\), and \\(i_j\\neq i_{j+1}\\) for \\(1\\leq j&lt;r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">As in original Definition 2.1, a freely generating Haar \\(d\\)-tuple in \\(M\\) is a tuple of unitaries \\(U=(U_1,\\ldots,U_d)\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  W^*(U)=M,\n  \\qquad\n  \\tau(U_g)=0\n  \\quad(g\\in\\mathbb F_d\\setminus\\{e\\}),\n  \\]\n  where \\(U_g\\) is the evaluation of the reduced group word \\(g\\), with inverse letters evaluated as adjoints.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a freely generating Haar \\(d\\)-tuple \\(U\\) in \\(M\\), the assignment\n\\[\n\\lambda(x_j)\\longmapsto U_j\n\\]\nextends to a unital normal trace-preserving \\(*\\)-isomorphism\n\\[\nL(\\mathbb F_d)\\longrightarrow M.\n\\]\nIn particular, two freely generating Haar tuples of the same size in \\(M\\) are related by a normal trace-preserving automorphism.</p><p>The component algebras of a Haar tuple are free. Freeness passes from unital \\(*\\)-algebras to their generated von Neumann algebras and persists when disjoint collections of free components are grouped.</p><p>For \\(d\\geq2\\), \\(L(\\mathbb F_d)\\) is a \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor.</p>", "proof": "<p>The vectors \\(U_g\\), indexed by \\(g\\in\\mathbb F_d\\), are orthonormal because\n\\[\n\\tau(U_h^*U_g)=\\tau(U_{h^{-1}g})=\\mathbf 1_{g=h}.\n\\]\nThey span \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\). Indeed, let \\(\\eta\\) be orthogonal to their span and choose \\(\\eta_m\\in M\\) converging to \\(\\eta\\) in \\(L^2\\). The normal functionals\n\\[\nf_m(x)=\\tau(\\eta_m^*x)\n\\]\nconverge in norm to \\(f(x)=\\langle x,\\eta\\rangle\\), since\n\\[\n\\|f_m-f\\|_{M^*}\\leq\\|\\eta_m-\\eta\\|_2.\n\\]\nThe space \\(M_*\\) of normal linear functionals is norm closed in \\(M^*\\), so \\(f\\) is normal. It vanishes on the ultraweakly dense word algebra and therefore on all of \\(M\\). Thus \\(\\eta=0\\).</p><p>Consequently,\n\\[\nV:\\ell^2(\\mathbb F_d)\\longrightarrow L^2(M,\\tau),\n\\qquad\nV\\delta_g=U_g,\n\\]\nis unitary. It intertwines \\(\\lambda(x_j)\\) with left multiplication by \\(U_j\\). Conjugation by \\(V\\) identifies their double commutants. The double commutant on the right is the faithful normal left representation of \\(W^*(U)=M\\), giving the claimed isomorphism. It preserves the trace because \\(V\\delta_e=1\\).</p><p>Applying these identifications to two freely generating Haar tuples gives the asserted automorphism.</p><p>The component algebras of a Haar tuple are free: expanding centered Laurent polynomials reduces the assertion to the nonidentity group-word trace, and bounded approximation gives freeness of the von Neumann closures. More generally, bounded strong approximation gives bounded \\(L^2\\) approximation in a finite tracial algebra; telescoping a product shows continuity of its trace under such approximations. Centering the approximants proves that freeness passes to von Neumann closures. Freeness also persists under grouping disjoint collections of components: their algebraic spans consist of scalars and reduced products of centered elements, and the same definition applies to the resulting reduced products.</p><p>Finally, for \\(d\\geq2\\), the canonical trace of \\(L(\\mathbb F_d)\\) is faithful and normal. Every nonidentity reduced word \\(g\\) has infinitely many distinct conjugates: choose a letter \\(a\\) whose powers cause no cancellation at either end of \\(a^kga^{-k}\\). At most two of the \\(2d\\) letters are excluded, and the reduced lengths then tend to infinity. Fourier coefficients of a central element are constant on conjugacy classes and form an \\(\\ell^2\\) vector. Every coefficient off the identity therefore vanishes. Faithfulness of the tracial representation implies that the center consists of scalars. The infinitely many orthonormal group unitaries show that the factor is infinite-dimensional.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Section 2.2 and Lemmas 2.3–2.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The logarithm satisfies \\( S=S^*,\\qquad \\|S\\|\\leq\\pi,\\qquad \\tau(S)=0,\\qquad e^{iS}=C,\\qquad W^*(S)=W^*(C). \\) The algebras \\(A_gW^*(C)A_g^*\\), \\(g\\in\\Gamma\\), form a free family.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((A_1,\\ldots,A_n,C)\\) be a freely generating Haar tuple in a finite tracial von Neumann algebra \\((M,\\tau)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\Gamma=\\mathbb F_n=\\langle x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\rangle\\), and let \\(A_g\\) be the word indexed by \\(g\\in\\Gamma\\) evaluated in \\(A_1,\\ldots,A_n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Using bounded Borel functional calculus, define\n  \\[\n  S=\\arg(C),\n  \\qquad\n  S_g=A_gSA_g^*\n  \\quad(g\\in\\Gamma),\n  \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]\n  where the argument takes values in \\((-\\pi,\\pi]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a monomial in \\(A_j,A_j^*,S\\), its group label is the product in \\(\\Gamma\\) of its group symbols, read in order with every occurrence of \\(S\\) ignored.</p><p class=\"li\">For a finitely supported real function \\(k\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\), put\n  \\[\n  s(k)=\\sum_{g\\in\\Gamma}k(g)S_g.\n  \\]\n  The coefficient norm \\(\\|k\\|_{\\ell^2}\\) is distinguished from the tracial norm \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The logarithm satisfies\n\\[\nS=S^*,\\qquad\n\\|S\\|\\leq\\pi,\\qquad\n\\tau(S)=0,\\qquad\ne^{iS}=C,\\qquad\nW^*(S)=W^*(C).\n\\]\nThe algebras \\(A_gW^*(C)A_g^*\\), \\(g\\in\\Gamma\\), form a free family. In particular, \\(S_g\\) is free from \\(W^*(S_b:b\\neq g)\\).</p><p>A monomial with nonidentity group label has trace zero. A monomial with identity label is a product of variables \\(S_g\\), or the constant \\(1\\).</p><p>For every finitely supported real \\(k\\),\n\\[\n\\|s(k)\\|\\leq K\\|k\\|_{\\ell^2},\n\\qquad K=3\\pi.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Bounded Borel functional calculus defines \\(S\\in W^*(C)\\). Since the spectral distribution of \\(C\\) is uniform on the circle,\n\\[\nS=S^*,\\quad\n\\|S\\|\\leq\\pi,\\quad\n\\tau(S)=0,\\quad\ne^{iS}=C,\\quad\nW^*(S)=W^*(C).\n\\]\nLemmas 2.1 and 2.2 give the freeness and group-label assertions.</p><p>Apply Lemma 2.3 to the finite family\n\\[\nz_g=k(g)S_g.\n\\]\nBoth \\(\\|S_g\\|_2\\) and \\(\\|S_g\\|\\) are at most \\(\\pi\\), and\n\\[\n\\max_g|k(g)|\\leq\\|k\\|_{\\ell^2}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\|s(k)\\|\n\\leq\n2\\pi\\|k\\|_{\\ell^2}+\\pi\\|k\\|_{\\ell^2}\n=3\\pi\\|k\\|_{\\ell^2}.\n\\]\nThis is original Lemma 2.5.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.3 (Freeness of the conjugate components)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The algebras \\( A_gW^*(C)A_g^*, \\qquad g\\in\\Gamma, \\) form a free family.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the freely generating Haar tuple, \\(\\Gamma\\), and \\(A_g\\) of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algebras\n\\[\nA_gW^*(C)A_g^*,\n\\qquad g\\in\\Gamma,\n\\]\nform a free family. In particular, each \\(S_g\\) is free from the von Neumann algebra generated by all \\(S_b\\) with \\(b\\neq g\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Consider a product of centered elements \\(A_{g_j}c_jA_{g_j}^*\\), with \\(c_j\\in W^*(C)\\) and \\(g_j\\neq g_{j+1}\\). Cyclicity rewrites its trace as\n\\[\n\\tau\\!\\left(\nc_1A_{g_1^{-1}g_2}c_2\\cdots\nA_{g_{r-1}^{-1}g_r}c_rA_{g_r^{-1}g_1}\n\\right).\n\\]\nAll interior group elements are nonidentity and hence centered. If the last group element is the identity, omit it; otherwise it too is centered. Freeness of \\(W^*(A_1,\\ldots,A_n)\\) and \\(W^*(C)\\), supplied by Proposition 1, makes this trace zero. For one factor, this is simply \\(\\tau(c_1)=0\\). The assertion about the other components follows by grouping free subalgebras.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.4 (Group labels and traces)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A monomial with nonidentity label has trace zero.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the setting and group-label convention of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A monomial with nonidentity label has trace zero. A monomial with identity label can be expressed as a product of variables \\(S_g\\), or as the constant \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first assertion, combine consecutive group symbols and consecutive powers of \\(S\\), and remove identity group factors. Split each polynomial in \\(S\\) into its scalar trace and a centered part. A term with all alternating factors centered has trace zero by freeness of the group algebra and \\(W^*(S)\\). Removing a scalar factor permits a reduction to fewer alternating factors without changing the total group label. Induction ends with either a centered alternating product or a single group element whose label remains nonidentity. Both have trace zero.</p><p>For the second assertion, successively use\n\\[\nA_gS=S_gA_g\n\\]\nto move every group factor to the right. The final group factor is exactly the label, and is therefore \\(1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "the general estimate proved inside Lemma 2.5 (Norm of a free sum)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\left\\|\\sum_jz_j\\right\\| \\leq 2\\left(\\sum_j\\|z_j\\|_2^2\\right)^{1/2} +\\max_j\\|z_j\\|. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this auxiliary estimate, replace the particular conjugate components of Proposition 2 by any finite free family of unital subalgebras in a finite tracial von Neumann algebra.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(z_j\\) be centered self-adjoint elements in the respective components.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_jz_j\\right\\|\n\\leq\n2\\left(\\sum_j\\|z_j\\|_2^2\\right)^{1/2}\n+\\max_j\\|z_j\\|.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Work in the tracial \\(L^2\\) space of the generated algebra. Freeness identifies this space with the orthogonal sum of the scalar vector and reduced centered tensor spaces whose successive component indices differ.</p><p>To check the identification, take inner products of reduced centered words. If the two letters at the joining point belong to different components, freeness gives zero. If they belong to the same component, split their product into its trace and centered part. The latter again has zero contribution, while the scalar term shortens both words. Iteration gives orthogonality for different index sequences and the tensor-product inner product for matching sequences. The reduced word span is dense by the density argument in Proposition 1.</p><p>Let \\(P_j\\) project onto the reduced tensors beginning with component \\(j\\), and let \\(L_{z_j}\\) denote left multiplication. On \\((1-P_j)L^2\\), multiplication prefixes \\(z_j\\), so\n\\[\nB_j=P_jL_{z_j}(1-P_j),\n\\qquad\n\\|B_j\\|\\leq\\|z_j\\|_2.\n\\]\nThe ranges of the \\(B_j\\) are orthogonal. Hence, for every vector \\(\\xi\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_jB_j\\xi\\right\\|^2\n=\\sum_j\\|B_j\\xi\\|^2\n\\leq\n\\left(\\sum_j\\|z_j\\|_2^2\\right)\\|\\xi\\|^2.\n\\]\nThe opposite blocks are \\(B_j^*\\), because \\(z_j\\) is self-adjoint. The block\n\\[\n(1-P_j)L_{z_j}(1-P_j)\n\\]\nis zero. The remaining diagonal blocks \\(P_jL_{z_j}P_j\\) act on mutually orthogonal subspaces; their sum has norm at most \\(\\max_j\\|z_j\\|\\). Summing these three parts proves the estimate, since the left representation is faithful.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 3.1 (Polynomial flow and displacement bounds)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a one-parameter group \\((\\beta_t)_{t\\in\\mathbb R}\\) of unital trace-preserving normal \\(*\\)-automorphisms of \\(M\\) fixing \\(S\\), whose restriction to \\(B\\) is point-norm continuous.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((A_1,\\ldots,A_n,C)\\) be a freely generating Haar tuple in a finite tracial von Neumann algebra \\((M,\\tau)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma=\\mathbb F_n=\\langle x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\rangle,\n  \\qquad\n  S=\\arg(C),\\qquad\n  S_g=A_gSA_g^*,\n  \\qquad\n  s(k)=\\sum_gk(g)S_g.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbb R^{(\\Gamma)}\\) be the finitely supported real functions on \\(\\Gamma\\), and let \\(\\delta_e\\in\\ell^2(\\Gamma)\\) be the unit vector at the identity.</p><p class=\"li\">The left regular action on coefficient vectors is\n  \\[\n  (\\lambda(g)k)(b)=k(g^{-1}b).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(h_1,\\ldots,h_n\\in\\mathbb R^{(\\Gamma)}\\). Let\n  \\(D:\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R^{(\\Gamma)}\\) be the unique cocycle satisfying\n  \\[\n  D_{x_j}=h_j,\n  \\qquad\n  D_{gb}=D_g+\\lambda(g)D_b.\n  \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]\n  Explicitly, set\n  \\[\n  D_{x_j^{-1}}=-\\lambda(x_j^{-1})h_j\n  \\]\n  and sum the shifted values of letters along a word. Terms associated with an adjacent inverse pair cancel, so this respects reduction.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  B=C^*(A_1,\\ldots,A_n,S),\n  \\qquad K=3\\pi.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a one-parameter group \\((\\beta_t)_{t\\in\\mathbb R}\\) of unital trace-preserving normal \\(*\\)-automorphisms of \\(M\\) fixing \\(S\\), whose restriction to \\(B\\) is point-norm continuous.</p><p>Writing \\(A_j(t)=\\beta_t(A_j)\\), and evaluating group words at this tuple, the paths satisfy, in operator norm,\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt}A_j(t)=i\\,s_t(h_j)A_j(t),\n\\qquad\ns_t(k)=\\sum_{g\\in\\Gamma}k(g)A_g(t)SA_g(t)^*,\n\\qquad\nA_j(0)=A_j.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nFor every \\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n\\[\n\\|\\beta_t(A_j)-A_j\\|\n\\leq |t|K\\|h_j\\|_{\\ell^2},\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|\\beta_t(A_w)-e^{itS}A_w\\|\n\\leq |t|K\\|D_w-\\delta_e\\|_{\\ell^2}\n\\qquad(w\\in\\Gamma).\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nAt \\(t=1\\), the target in the second estimate is \\(CA_w\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the solution \\(A(t)\\) of Lemma 3.2 in \\(B\\), and its moment invariance from Lemma 3.3.</p><p>For fixed \\(t\\), define\n\\[\n\\phi_t:\\operatorname{ev}_0(\\mathcal P)\\longrightarrow B,\n\\qquad\n\\phi_t(\\operatorname{ev}_0(p))=\\operatorname{ev}_t(p).\n\\]\nThis is well defined. If \\(\\operatorname{ev}_0(p)=0\\), moment invariance for \\(p^*p\\) gives\n\\[\n\\tau\\!\\left(\\operatorname{ev}_t(p)^*\n                  \\operatorname{ev}_t(p)\\right)=0,\n\\]\nso faithfulness implies \\(\\operatorname{ev}_t(p)=0\\). Thus additional relations at the initial evaluated tuple are respected as a consequence of actual moment invariance. Formal evaluation makes \\(\\phi_t\\) unital, complex-linear, multiplicative, and adjoint-preserving.</p><p>For an element \\(b\\) of a finite von Neumann algebra with faithful tracial state,\n\\[\n\\|b\\|=\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\\tau((b^*b)^k)^{1/(2k)}.\n\\]\nThe upper bound is immediate; for the lower bound, every nonzero spectral projection of \\(b^*b\\) sufficiently near its norm has positive trace by faithfulness. Applying moment invariance to \\((p^*p)^k\\) shows that \\(\\phi_t\\) is isometric. It extends uniquely to a trace-preserving isometric \\(*\\)-homomorphism on \\(B\\), with range\n\\[\nB_t=C^*(A_1(t),\\ldots,A_n(t),S).\n\\]\nIts range is closed and is the norm closure of the polynomials in these time-\\(t\\) generators.</p><p>To show \\(B_t=B\\), apply Lemma 3.2 inside \\(B_t\\) with initial tuple \\(A(t)\\), the same fixed \\(S\\), and the same field. Viewed in \\(M\\), its global solution must be\n\\[\nu\\longmapsto A(t+u)\n\\]\nby uniqueness. At \\(u=-t\\), it recovers the original tuple. Thus every \\(A_j\\) belongs to \\(B_t\\), and \\(S\\) already belongs to \\(B_t\\), giving \\(B\\subseteq B_t\\). The forward construction gives the reverse inclusion. Hence \\(\\phi_t\\) is a \\(C^*\\)-automorphism of \\(B\\) fixing \\(S\\).</p><p>Since \\(e^{iS}=C\\), the von Neumann algebra generated by \\(B\\) is \\(M\\). Its vectors are dense in \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\), by the density argument in Proposition 1. Trace preservation and surjectivity define a unitary\n\\[\nU_t[b]=[\\phi_t(b)]\n\\qquad(b\\in B)\n\\]\non \\(L^2(M,\\tau)\\), fixing \\([1]\\). For left multiplication \\(L_b\\),\n\\[\nU_tL_bU_t^*=L_{\\phi_t(b)}\n\\qquad(b\\in B).\n\\]\nThe faithful normal left representation identifies the von Neumann closure of \\(L(B)\\) with \\(M\\). Conjugation by \\(U_t\\) therefore gives a normal \\(*\\)-automorphism \\(\\beta_t\\) of \\(M\\) extending \\(\\phi_t\\). It is trace-preserving because \\(U_t\\) fixes \\([1]\\), and it fixes \\(S\\). Ultraweak density of \\(B\\) makes this normal extension unique.</p><p>For fixed \\(s\\), the path\n\\[\nt\\longmapsto\\phi_s(A(t))\n\\]\nsatisfies the same polynomial equation, since \\(\\phi_s\\) is a bounded \\(*\\)-homomorphism fixing \\(S\\). Its initial tuple is \\(A(s)\\), so uniqueness gives\n\\[\n\\phi_s(A_j(t))=A_j(s+t).\n\\]\nEquality on the generators and on \\(S\\) yields\n\\[\n\\phi_s\\phi_t=\\phi_{s+t}\n\\]\non \\(B\\), and uniqueness of normal extension gives\n\\[\n\\beta_s\\beta_t=\\beta_{s+t}\n\\]\non \\(M\\). Also \\(\\beta_0\\) is the identity. Norm continuity on polynomials follows from the solution paths; density and the common isometry bound extend it to point-norm continuity on \\(B\\). No point-norm continuity on arbitrary elements of \\(M\\) is needed.</p><p>For a finitely supported real \\(k\\),\n\\[\ns_t(k)=\\beta_t(s(k)),\n\\qquad\n\\|s_t(k)\\|=\\|s(k)\\|\\leq K\\|k\\|_{\\ell^2}\n\\]\nby Proposition 2. Integrating\n\\[\n\\|A_j'(t)\\|\\leq K\\|h_j\\|_{\\ell^2}\n\\]\nbetween zero and \\(t\\) gives the first displacement bound.</p><p>For \\(w\\in\\Gamma\\), Lemma 3.1 evaluated along the flow gives\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt}A_w(t)=i\\,s_t(D_w)A_w(t).\n\\]\nMoreover \\(s_t(\\delta_e)=S\\). Keeping the order of factors,\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(e^{-itS}A_w(t)\\bigr)\n=i e^{-itS}\\bigl(s_t(D_w)-S\\bigr)A_w(t).\n\\]\nThe exterior factors are unitary, so the derivative has norm at most\n\\[\nK\\|D_w-\\delta_e\\|_{\\ell^2}.\n\\]\nIntegration between zero and \\(t\\), followed by multiplication on the left by \\(e^{itS}\\), proves the second displacement bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2 (Universal initial trace identity)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The rules define a \\(*\\)-derivation on \\(\\mathcal P\\), and \\( \\delta a_g=i\\sigma(D_g)a_g, \\qquad \\delta z_g=i[\\sigma(D_g),z_g]. \\) For every \\(p\\in\\mathcal P\\) and integer \\(r\\geq1\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the initial Haar tuple, \\(S\\), \\(\\Gamma\\), coefficient vectors, and cocycle of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal P\\) be the complex unital \\(*\\)-algebra generated by unitaries \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\) and a self-adjoint symbol \\(z\\), subject only to these unitary and self-adjoint relations.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(g\\in\\Gamma\\), let \\(a_g\\) be the corresponding formal group word, and put\n  \\[\n  z_g=a_gza_g^*,\n  \\qquad\n  \\sigma(k)=\\sum_gk(g)z_g.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{ev}_0:\\mathcal P\\to B\n  \\]\n  evaluate \\(a_j\\) at \\(A_j\\) and \\(z\\) at \\(S\\). No assumption is made about its kernel.</p><p class=\"li\">Define a complex-linear derivation by\n  \\[\n  \\delta z=0,\n  \\qquad\n  \\delta a_j=i\\sigma(h_j)a_j,\n  \\qquad\n  \\delta a_j^*=-ia_j^*\\sigma(h_j).\n  \\tag{3.5}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rules define a \\(*\\)-derivation on \\(\\mathcal P\\), and\n\\[\n\\delta a_g=i\\sigma(D_g)a_g,\n\\qquad\n\\delta z_g=i[\\sigma(D_g),z_g].\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nFor every \\(p\\in\\mathcal P\\) and integer \\(r\\geq1\\),\n\\[\n\\tau\\!\\left(\\operatorname{ev}_0(\\delta^rp)\\right)=0.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The reality of the \\(h_j\\) makes \\(\\sigma(h_j)\\) self-adjoint, so the rules respect adjoints. They respect the unitary relations: for example,\n\\[\n\\delta(a_j^*a_j)\n=-ia_j^*\\sigma(h_j)a_j+ia_j^*\\sigma(h_j)a_j=0,\n\\]\nand the calculation for \\(a_ja_j^*\\) is the same. Thus they define a \\(*\\)-derivation.</p><p>The identity\n\\[\na_g\\sigma(k)a_g^*=\\sigma(\\lambda(g)k)\n\\]\nand the cocycle rule give\n\\[\n\\delta a_g=i\\sigma(D_g)a_g,\n\\qquad\n\\delta z_g=i[\\sigma(D_g),z_g].\n\\]\nFor example, multiplying the formulas for \\(g\\) and \\(b\\) gives the formula for \\(gb\\), with coefficient \\(D_g+\\lambda(g)D_b\\); inverse letters are covered by the unitary relations.</p><p>First prove the trace identity for \\(r=1\\). Give a monomial its group label by ignoring occurrences of \\(z\\) and multiplying the remaining group symbols. Each term of \\(\\sigma(k)\\) has label \\(e\\). The replacement rules therefore preserve the label term by term. If the label is nonidentity, Proposition 2 makes every evaluated derivative term have trace zero.</p><p>An identity-label monomial is a polynomial in the \\(z_g\\). Algebraically, repeatedly use\n\\[\na_gz=z_ga_g\n\\]\nto move group factors to the right; the final group factor is the identity. These are identities in \\(\\mathcal P\\), and \\(\\delta\\) is a derivation there, so differentiating this rewriting gives the same result as differentiating the original monomial. It suffices to consider\n\\[\np=F(z_g:g\\in I)\n\\]\nfor finite \\(I\\subset\\Gamma\\).</p><p>Fix \\(g\\in I\\). Remove the commuting own-variable term from the commutator. Its evaluated direction is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ev}_0(\\delta z_g)=i[Y_g,S_g],\n\\qquad\nY_g=\\sum_{b\\neq g}D_g(b)S_b.\n\\]\nThe element \\(Y_g\\) is bounded and self-adjoint in\n\\[\nB_g=W^*(S_b:b\\neq g),\n\\]\nand \\(W^*(S_g)\\) is free from \\(B_g\\), by Proposition 2.</p><p>If a unital algebra \\(\\mathcal A\\) is free from a unital algebra \\(\\mathcal B\\), and \\(U\\in\\mathcal B\\) is unitary, then \\(U\\mathcal A U^*\\) is free from \\(\\mathcal B\\). Indeed, conjugating an alternating centered product by \\(U^*\\) gives an alternating centered product from \\(\\mathcal A\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\): a centered \\(b\\in\\mathcal B\\) becomes \\(U^*bU\\), which is still centered. Its trace is zero. Conjugation preserves marginal laws, and freeness determines mixed moments from marginal laws. Replacing \\(S_g\\) by \\(US_gU^*\\) therefore preserves its joint law with any fixed collection of elements of \\(B_g\\).</p><p>Apply this with \\(U=e^{iuY_g}\\), for real \\(u\\). Replacing every occurrence of \\(S_g\\) in \\(F\\) by\n\\[\ne^{iuY_g}S_ge^{-iuY_g}\n\\]\nand leaving all other variables fixed gives a scalar function with constant trace. Its derivative at zero is the trace of the sum over those occurrences with \\(i[Y_g,S_g]\\) inserted. Sum these zero derivatives over \\(g\\in I\\). The product rule and the formula for \\(\\delta z_g\\) identify the sum with\n\\[\n\\tau(\\operatorname{ev}_0(\\delta p)).\n\\]\nLinearity handles arbitrary finite sums of monomials.</p><p>Thus\n\\[\n\\tau\\circ\\operatorname{ev}_0\\circ\\delta=0\n\\]\non all of \\(\\mathcal P\\). Applying this identity to \\(\\delta^{r-1}p\\), which is again a finite polynomial, gives the result for every \\(r\\geq1\\). Every derivative of every coefficient polynomial is included. The auxiliary conjugations compute only a first variation at the initial tuple; they need neither lift to motions of the \\(a_j\\) nor be jointly integrated.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3 (Global solvability in a generated subalgebra)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The equation \\( X'=\\mathcal V(X), \\qquad X(0)=V, \\) has a unique global solution in \\(E^n\\), smooth in norm, and all its coordinates remain unitary.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the finite tracial algebra \\(M\\), the fixed bounded self-adjoint element \\(S\\), and the finitely supported real vectors \\(h_1,\\ldots,h_n\\) from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">For this auxiliary assertion, the initial tuple need not be Haar or generating.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\subset M\\) be any closed unital \\(C^*\\)-subalgebra containing \\(S\\), and let \\(V_1,\\ldots,V_n\\) be unitaries in \\(E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_n)\\in E^n\\), let \\(X_g\\) be the reduced group word evaluated using adjoints for inverse letters, even when the \\(X_j\\) are not unitary. Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal V_j(X)=iH_j(X)X_j,\n  \\qquad\n  H_j(X)=\\sum_g h_j(g)X_gSX_g^*.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The equation\n\\[\nX'=\\mathcal V(X),\n\\qquad\nX(0)=V,\n\\]\nhas a unique global solution in \\(E^n\\), smooth in norm, and all its coordinates remain unitary.</p>", "proof": "<p>Regard \\(E^n\\), with its maximum norm, as a real Banach space. Adjunction is bounded and real-linear, so \\(\\mathcal V\\) is a finite smooth polynomial map. Its norm and Lipschitz constant are bounded on every fixed bounded ball. Picard iteration gives a unique local solution, and repeated differentiation makes it smooth.</p><p>Each \\(H_j(X)\\) is self-adjoint, even before unitarity is known. Along a local solution,\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt}(X_j^*X_j)\n=-iX_j^*H_j(X)X_j+iX_j^*H_j(X)X_j=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(X_j^*X_j=1\\). An isometry in \\(M\\) is unitary: the projection \\(1-X_jX_j^*\\) has trace\n\\[\n1-\\tau(X_j^*X_j)=0,\n\\]\nand faithfulness makes it zero. This equality also holds in \\(E\\), so \\(\\|X_j\\|=1\\) throughout the local solution.</p><p>On this unitary trajectory,\n\\[\n\\|X_j'\\|\\leq\\|S\\|\\sum_g|h_j(g)|.\n\\]\nIf a maximal interval had a finite endpoint, the bounded derivative would make the tuple norm-Cauchy as time approached that endpoint. Its limit lies in \\(E\\) and is again a unitary tuple. Local existence at the limit continues the solution, a contradiction. The same argument applies to either endpoint, giving a global solution. Uniqueness follows from local uniqueness on overlapping intervals. This continuation uses completeness and bounded derivative, not compactness of a bounded ball.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.4 (Moment invariance)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(p\\in\\mathcal P\\) and \\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\), \\( \\tau(\\operatorname{ev}_t(p)) =\\tau(\\operatorname{ev}_0(p)). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Return to the freely generating Haar initial tuple of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Apply Lemma 3.2 with\n  \\[\n  E=B=C^*(A_1,\\ldots,A_n,S),\n  \\qquad\n  X(0)=(A_1,\\ldots,A_n),\n  \\]\n  and write \\(A(t)\\) for the resulting global unitary solution.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal P\\) be the formal unital \\(*\\)-algebra on unitary symbols \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\) and a self-adjoint symbol \\(z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\sigma(k)=\\sum_g k(g)a_gza_g^*,\n  \\]\n  and let \\(\\delta\\) be the derivation\n  \\[\n  \\delta z=0,\\qquad\n  \\delta a_j=i\\sigma(h_j)a_j,\\qquad\n  \\delta a_j^*=-ia_j^*\\sigma(h_j).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\operatorname{ev}_t:\\mathcal P\\to B\\) evaluate the formal generators at \\(A(t),S\\). Unitarity makes this well defined.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(p\\in\\mathcal P\\) and \\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n\\[\n\\tau(\\operatorname{ev}_t(p))\n=\\tau(\\operatorname{ev}_0(p)).\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The equation and product rule give, for every integer \\(r\\geq0\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{d^r}{dt^r}\\operatorname{ev}_t(p)\n=\\operatorname{ev}_t(\\delta^rp).\n\\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nWe verify that these derivatives give a convergent Taylor expansion for the trace.</p><p>Choose fixed polynomial representatives for the derivatives of the formal letters. There are constants \\(J\\geq1\\) and \\(B_1\\geq1\\) such that replacing one letter by its derivative increases monomial length by at most \\(J\\) and produces total absolute coefficient sum at most \\(B_1\\). These constants are finite because the \\(h_j\\) have finite support.</p><p>If a representative of \\(p\\) has degree at most \\(l\\geq1\\) and total absolute coefficient sum \\(b_0\\), repeated replacement gives a representative of \\(\\delta^rp\\) with degree at most \\(l+rJ\\) and coefficient sum at most\n\\[\nb_0B_1^r\\prod_{v=0}^{r-1}(l+vJ).\n\\]\nLet \\(R=\\max(1,\\|S\\|)\\). All evaluated group letters are unitary. Uniformly for real \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac1{r!}\n\\left\\|\n\\frac{d^r}{dt^r}\\operatorname{ev}_t(p)\n\\right\\|\n&amp;\\leq\nb_0R^l(B_1R^J)^r\n\\frac{\\prod_{v=0}^{r-1}(l+vJ)}{r!}\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\nb_0R^l\\bigl[B_1R^J(l+J)\\bigr]^r.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(3.10)\n\\]\nThe last inequality uses \\(l+vJ\\leq(l+J)(v+1)\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nf_p(t)=\\tau(\\operatorname{ev}_t(p)),\n\\qquad\nc_p=B_1R^J(l+J).\n\\]\nThe integral form of Taylor’s remainder bounds the remainder after degree \\(r\\), at increment \\(u\\), by\n\\[\nb_0R^l(c_p|u|)^{r+1}.\n\\]\nThus \\(f_p\\) is real-analytic near every real time, with radius at least \\(c_p^{-1}&gt;0\\). Lemma 3.1 makes every positive-order derivative at zero vanish. Hence \\(f_p\\) is constant near zero and, by real-analytic continuation on the connected line \\(\\mathbb R\\), constant everywhere.</p><p>The constants may depend on the fixed field and on \\(p\\). No radius common to all polynomials or different choices of the \\(h_j\\) is required. Only unitarity of the solution and boundedness of \\(S\\) were used; freeness at positive times was not assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 4.1 (Small coefficients with a prescribed word value)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist \\(w\\in\\Gamma\\) and a finitely supported real coefficient vector \\(h\\) such that the cocycle determined by \\( h_1=h,\\qquad h_j=0\\quad(2\\leq j\\leq n) \\) satisfies …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq3\\),\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma=\\mathbb F_n=\\langle x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\rangle,\n  \\qquad\n  (\\lambda(g)h)(b)=h(g^{-1}b).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\delta_e\\in\\ell^2(\\Gamma)\\) be the unit vector at the identity.</p><p class=\"li\">For finitely supported real generator coefficients \\(h_1,\\ldots,h_n\\), let \\(D\\) be the cocycle\n  \\[\n  D_{x_j}=h_j,\n  \\qquad\n  D_{gb}=D_g+\\lambda(g)D_b.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\eta&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(w\\in\\Gamma\\) and a finitely supported real coefficient vector \\(h\\) such that the cocycle determined by\n\\[\nh_1=h,\\qquad h_j=0\\quad(2\\leq j\\leq n)\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\|h\\|_{\\ell^2}&lt;\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\|D_w-\\delta_e\\|_{\\ell^2}&lt;\\eta.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use \\(T_m,Q_m,\\mu_m\\) from Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2. Since \\(\\nu(\\{0\\})=0\\), choose a fixed \\(d&gt;0\\) so small that\n\\[\n\\nu([0,d])&lt;\\frac{\\eta^2}{16}.\n\\]\nThe interval \\([0,d]\\) is closed, so weak convergence and the Portmanteau inequality give\n\\[\n\\limsup_{m\\to\\infty}\\mu_m([0,d])\n\\leq\\nu([0,d])&lt;\\frac{\\eta^2}{16}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(m\\) sufficiently large that\n\\[\n\\mu_m([0,d])&lt;\\frac{\\eta^2}{8},\n\\qquad\n\\frac1{dm}&lt;\\frac{\\eta^2}{16}.\n\\tag{4.10}\n\\]\nKeep \\(d,m\\) fixed.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nE_m=1_{(dm,\\infty)}(Q_m),\n\\qquad\nR_m=f_m(Q_m),\n\\qquad\nf_m(x)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&amp;0\\leq x\\leq dm,\\\\\nx^{-1},&amp;x&gt;dm.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThen \\(R_m\\) is positive and bounded, with \\(\\|R_m\\|\\leq(dm)^{-1}\\), and\n\\[\nQ_mR_m=E_m,\\qquad\nR_mQ_mR_m=R_m,\\qquad\n\\tau_\\Gamma(1-E_m)=\\mu_m([0,d]).\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nThese operations allow a kernel or arbitrarily small nonzero spectrum of \\(Q_m\\).</p><p>Define\n\\[\nh^{(0)}=T_m^*R_m\\delta_e\\in\\ell^2(\\Gamma).\n\\]\nUsing \\(Q_m=T_mT_m^*\\) in this order gives\n\\[\nT_mh^{(0)}=E_m\\delta_e,\n\\tag{4.12}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|T_mh^{(0)}-\\delta_e\\|_{\\ell^2}^2\n=\\tau_\\Gamma(1-E_m)&lt;\\frac{\\eta^2}{8},\n\\tag{4.13}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|h^{(0)}\\|_{\\ell^2}^2\n&amp;=\\langle R_mQ_mR_m\\delta_e,\\delta_e\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\tau_\\Gamma(R_m)\n\\leq\\frac1{dm}&lt;\\frac{\\eta^2}{16}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(4.14)\n\\]</p><p>The vector \\(h^{(0)}\\) has real coefficients. Let \\(J\\) be coefficientwise complex conjugation on \\(\\ell^2(\\Gamma)\\). Each left shift is real in the standard basis, so \\(J\\) commutes with \\(T_m,T_m^*,Q_m\\), and \\(J\\delta_e=\\delta_e\\). Commutation with real polynomials in \\(Q_m\\) extends by uniform approximation to real continuous functions of \\(Q_m\\). The functions\n\\[\nx\\longmapsto\\min\\{1,\\max\\{0,l(x-dm)\\}\\},\n\\qquad l=1,2,\\ldots,\n\\]\nconverge pointwise, with a uniform bound, to \\(1_{(dm,\\infty)}(x)\\). Their functional calculi converge strongly to \\(E_m\\), so \\(J\\) also commutes with \\(E_m\\). Finally,\n\\[\nR_m=E_mg_m(Q_m),\n\\qquad\ng_m(x)=\\frac1{\\max\\{dm,x\\}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(g_m\\) is real and continuous on \\([0,\\infty)\\). Hence \\(J\\) commutes with \\(R_m\\), proving \\(Jh^{(0)}=h^{(0)}\\).</p><p>Real finitely supported vectors are dense in the real subspace of \\(\\ell^2(\\Gamma)\\). Choose one such vector \\(h\\) with\n\\[\n\\|h-h^{(0)}\\|_{\\ell^2}&lt;\\frac{\\eta}{4(m+1)}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\|T_m\\|\\leq m+1\\),\n\\[\n\\|h\\|_{\\ell^2}\n&lt;\\frac{\\eta}{4}+\\frac{\\eta}{4(m+1)}\n&lt;\\eta\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|T_mh-\\delta_e\\|_{\\ell^2}\n&lt;\\frac{\\eta}{\\sqrt8}+\\frac{\\eta}{4}\n&lt;\\eta.\n\\]\nForm the cocycle with \\(h_1=h\\) and the other generator coefficients zero, and take \\(w=w_m\\). Lemma 4.1 gives \\(D_w=T_mh\\), proving the assertion.</p><p>The choices were made in the order \\(d\\), then \\(m\\), then the finite coefficient approximation. The final approximation uses the norm of the already fixed \\(T_m\\); no support bound uniform in \\(\\eta\\) is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.2 (Free prefixes and the cocycle value)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(m\\geq1\\), the elements \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_m\\) freely generate a free subgroup of \\(\\Gamma\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(n\\geq3\\), \\(\\Gamma\\), and its free generators from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j\\geq1\\), set\n  \\[\n  a=x_1,\\qquad\n  b_j=x_2^jx_3x_2^{-j},\n  \\qquad\n  p_0=e,\\qquad\n  p_j=(ab_1)\\cdots(ab_j).\n  \\tag{4.2}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(m\\geq1\\), set\n  \\[\n  w_m=p_ma,\n  \\qquad\n  T_m=1+\\sum_{j=1}^m\\lambda(p_j).\n  \\tag{4.3}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(m\\geq1\\), the elements \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_m\\) freely generate a free subgroup of \\(\\Gamma\\). If \\(h_1=h\\) and \\(h_j=0\\) for \\(j\\geq2\\), then\n\\[\nD_{w_m}=T_mh.\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Consider a nonempty reduced word in \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\). Combining consecutive powers with the same index writes it as\n\\[\nb_{j_1}^{q_1}\\cdots b_{j_l}^{q_l},\n\\qquad\nq_i\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\},\n\\qquad\nj_i\\neq j_{i+1}.\n\\]\nIts expansion is\n\\[\nx_2^{j_1}x_3^{q_1}\nx_2^{j_2-j_1}x_3^{q_2}\n\\cdots\nx_2^{j_l-j_{l-1}}x_3^{q_l}x_2^{-j_l}.\n\\]\nEvery displayed \\(x_3\\) power and each intervening \\(x_2\\) power is nonzero, so this cannot reduce to the identity. Thus the \\(b_j\\) freely generate their subgroup.</p><p>The decomposition\n\\[\n\\Gamma=\\langle a\\rangle*\\langle x_2,\\ldots,x_n\\rangle\n\\]\nthen shows that \\(a,b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\) freely generate their subgroup: an alternating product of nonidentity elements from \\(\\langle a\\rangle\\) and \\(\\langle b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\rangle\\) remains reduced in this free product.</p><p>In the abstract free group on \\(a,b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\), the substitution fixing \\(a\\) and sending \\(b_j\\) to \\(ab_j\\) has inverse fixing \\(a\\) and sending \\(b_j\\) to \\(a^{-1}b_j\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nu_j=ab_j,\\qquad1\\leq j\\leq m,\n\\]\nform a free family.</p><p>In the abstract free group on \\(u_1,\\ldots,u_m\\), define\n\\[\n\\varphi(u_j)=u_1\\cdots u_j,\n\\qquad\n\\psi(u_1)=u_1,\n\\qquad\n\\psi(u_j)=u_{j-1}^{-1}u_j\\quad(j\\geq2).\n\\]\nCancellation gives\n\\[\n\\psi\\varphi(u_j)\n=u_1(u_1^{-1}u_2)\\cdots(u_{j-1}^{-1}u_j)\n=u_j\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\varphi\\psi(u_j)\n=(u_1\\cdots u_{j-1})^{-1}(u_1\\cdots u_j)\n=u_j\n\\quad(j\\geq2).\n\\]\nThe second identity also holds for \\(j=1\\). Hence \\(\\varphi\\) and \\(\\psi\\) are inverse automorphisms. Since \\(p_j=u_1\\cdots u_j\\), the \\(p_j\\) form a free family.</p><p>For the cocycle assertion, the inverse rule\n\\[\nD_{x_j^{-1}}=-\\lambda(x_j^{-1})D_{x_j}\n\\]\nshows that \\(D\\) vanishes on \\(\\langle x_2,\\ldots,x_n\\rangle\\). In particular, \\(D_{b_j}=0\\). The positive occurrences of \\(a\\) in \\(w_m\\) have prefixes \\(p_0,p_1,\\ldots,p_m\\), and there are no other occurrences of \\(a\\) or \\(a^{-1}\\). Successive use of the cocycle identity gives\n\\[\nD_{w_m}=\\sum_{j=0}^m\\lambda(p_j)h=T_mh.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.3 (Spectral limit of the prefix sums)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The measures \\(\\mu_m\\) converge weakly to the probability measure \\( d\\nu(x)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\frac{4-x}{x}}\\, 1_{(0,4)}(x)\\,dx. \\) In particular, \\(\\nu(\\{0\\})=0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the prefix words and operators \\(T_m\\) of Lemma 4.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\tau_\\Gamma(X)=\\langle X\\delta_e,\\delta_e\\rangle\n  \\]\n  be the canonical trace on \\(L(\\Gamma)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  Q_m=T_mT_m^*,\n  \\qquad\n  \\mu_m(B)=\\tau_\\Gamma\\bigl(1_B(Q_m/m)\\bigr)\n  \\tag{4.5}\n  \\]\n  for Borel \\(B\\subseteq[0,\\infty)\\). These are probability measures.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The measures \\(\\mu_m\\) converge weakly to the probability measure\n\\[\nd\\nu(x)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\frac{4-x}{x}}\\,\n1_{(0,4)}(x)\\,dx.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(\\nu(\\{0\\})=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nV_j=\\lambda(p_j)\\quad(1\\leq j\\leq m),\n\\qquad V_0=1.\n\\]\nBy Lemma 4.1, a word in \\(V_1,\\ldots,V_m\\) and their inverses has trace one if its word in these free symbols reduces to the identity, and trace zero otherwise.</p><p>This rule applies inside the given regular representation. For\n\\[\nH=\\langle p_1,\\ldots,p_m\\rangle,\n\\]\nthe spaces \\(\\ell^2(Ht)\\), as \\(Ht\\) ranges over right cosets, are reducing copies of the left regular representation of \\(H\\). The vector \\(\\delta_e\\) lies in \\(\\ell^2(H)\\). Its state therefore agrees with the canonical trace of \\(H\\) on polynomials and on the spectral projections under consideration.</p><p>Fix \\(r\\geq1\\). Expanding \\(Q_m^r\\) gives\n\\[\n\\tau_\\Gamma(Q_m^r)\n=\n\\sum_{i_1,\\ldots,i_{2r}\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m\\}}\n\\tau_\\Gamma(V_{i_1}V_{i_2}^*\\cdots\nV_{i_{2r-1}}V_{i_{2r}}^*).\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nEach summand is zero or one. In a summand equal to one, every nonzero index occurs at least twice, since a generator occurring once cannot disappear under free reduction. Thus at most \\(r\\) distinct nonzero indices occur.</p><p>If exactly \\(r\\) occur, all \\(2r\\) positions are nonidentity and every index occurs exactly twice. Its two occurrences have opposite exponents, as follows by abelianizing the identity word. Pair the positions with the same index; this matching is unique. Adjacent inverse cancellations produce a noncrossing matching: a pair deleted after other pairs can only enclose pairs already deleted, and deleted pairs cannot cross.</p><p>Conversely, a noncrossing matching with opposite exponents at its endpoints reduces to the identity by deleting an innermost pair and proceeding by induction. Every noncrossing perfect matching of \\(\\{1,\\ldots,2r\\}\\) has opposite parity at each pair’s endpoints, because the positions strictly inside that pair are matched among themselves and hence number an even integer. Since the signs in the expansion alternate, every such matching has the required opposite signs.</p><p>The number of noncrossing perfect matchings is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Cat}_r=\\frac1{r+1}\\binom{2r}{r}.\n\\]\nIndeed, splitting at the partner of the first position gives\n\\[\nc_0=1,\\qquad\nc_r=\\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}c_jc_{r-1-j}.\n\\]\nThe generating series satisfies \\(C(z)=1+zC(z)^2\\), whose solution with constant term one is\n\\[\nC(z)=\\frac{1-\\sqrt{1-4z}}{2z};\n\\]\nthe binomial expansion gives the displayed coefficient.</p><p>For each matching, labeling its \\(r\\) pairs with distinct indices from \\(\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}\\) gives\n\\[\n(m)_r=m(m-1)\\cdots(m-r+1)\n\\]\nchoices, with value zero if \\(m&lt;r\\). Order pairs by their first endpoints; uniqueness of the equal-index matching prevents double counting. The contribution from exactly \\(r\\) distinct indices is therefore \\(\\operatorname{Cat}_r(m)_r\\).</p><p>Every remaining contributing term has at most \\(r-1\\) distinct nonzero indices. This includes every term with an identity summand: it has at most \\(2r-1\\) nonidentity positions, and each index occurs at least twice. For fixed \\(r\\), only finitely many patterns specify the identity positions and the partition of remaining positions into equal-index classes. A pattern with \\(k\\leq r-1\\) classes has at most \\(m^k\\) labelings. Thus\n\\[\n\\tau_\\Gamma(Q_m^r)\n=\\operatorname{Cat}_r(m)_r+O_r(m^{r-1}),\n\\qquad\n\\int x^r\\,d\\mu_m(x)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Cat}_r.\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(r=1\\), direct expansion gives \\(\\tau_\\Gamma(Q_m)=m+1\\), so the first moments of \\(\\mu_m\\) are bounded by two. Markov’s inequality gives tightness on \\([0,\\infty)\\). Moment convergence at \\(r+1\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sup_m\\int x^{r+1}\\,d\\mu_m(x)&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sup_m\\int_{(R,\\infty)}x^r\\,d\\mu_m(x)\n\\leq\n\\frac1R\\sup_m\\int x^{r+1}\\,d\\mu_m(x)\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nIf a subsequence converges weakly to a probability measure \\(\\mu\\), apply weak convergence to\n\\(\\min\\{x^r,R^r\\}\\) and use this tail bound. The corresponding bound for \\(\\mu\\) follows by applying weak convergence first to bounded truncations of \\(x^{r+1}\\). Letting \\(R\\to\\infty\\) gives\n\\[\n\\int x^r\\,d\\mu(x)=\\operatorname{Cat}_r\n\\qquad(r\\geq0).\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]</p><p>The proposed density realizes these moments, including the zeroth. Writing\n\\[\nB(a,b)=\\int_0^1t^{a-1}(1-t)^{b-1}\\,dt,\n\\]\nthe substitution \\(x=4t\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^4x^{r-1/2}(4-x)^{1/2}\\,dx\n&amp;=\\frac{4^{r+1}}{2\\pi}\nB\\!\\left(r+\\frac12,\\frac32\\right)\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{(2r)!}{r!(r+1)!}\n=\\operatorname{Cat}_r.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe middle equality follows from \\(B(\\frac12,\\frac32)=\\pi/2\\) and\n\\[\nB(a+1,b)=\\frac{a}{a+b}B(a,b).\n\\]\nIn particular, the density has total mass one.</p><p>These moments uniquely determine a probability measure on \\([0,\\infty)\\). Since \\(\\operatorname{Cat}_r\\leq4^r\\), mass \\(c&gt;0\\) on \\([4+\\epsilon,\\infty)\\) would imply\n\\[\n4^r\\geq\\operatorname{Cat}_r\n\\geq c(4+\\epsilon)^r\n\\]\nfor every \\(r\\), a contradiction. The support is therefore contained in \\([0,4]\\). Uniform polynomial approximation on this compact interval shows that equality of all moments implies equality of measures.</p><p>Every subsequential weak limit is consequently \\(\\nu\\), and tightness gives convergence of the full sequence. The displayed integrable density has no atom at zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 5.1 (Approximating the extra generator in one step)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are a trace-preserving normal automorphism \\(\\alpha\\) of \\(M\\) and \\(w\\in\\mathbb F_n\\) such that, with \\(A_j'=\\alpha(A_j)\\), \\( \\max_{1\\leq j\\leq n}\\|A_j'-A_j\\|<\\epsilon, \\qquad \\|A_w'-C\\|<\\epsilon. \\) The tuple …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq3\\), and let \\((A_1,\\ldots,A_n,C)\\) be a freely generating Haar tuple in a finite tracial von Neumann algebra \\((M,\\tau)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(w\\in\\mathbb F_n\\), let \\(A_w\\) be its evaluation on the first \\(n\\) coordinates.</p><p class=\"li\">A unitary \\(C\\) is called a completion of \\(A=(A_1,\\ldots,A_n)\\) when \\((A,C)\\) is a freely generating Haar tuple.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a trace-preserving normal automorphism \\(\\alpha\\) of \\(M\\) and \\(w\\in\\mathbb F_n\\) such that, with \\(A_j'=\\alpha(A_j)\\),\n\\[\n\\max_{1\\leq j\\leq n}\\|A_j'-A_j\\|&lt;\\epsilon,\n\\qquad\n\\|A_w'-C\\|&lt;\\epsilon.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nThe tuple \\((A_1',\\ldots,A_n',C')\\), where \\(C'=\\alpha(C)\\), is again freely generating Haar. The target in the second estimate is the original \\(C\\), and the new completion is exactly\n\\[\nC'=CA_w^*,\n\\]\nwhere \\(A_w\\) is evaluated on the original tuple.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;\\epsilon/K\\), where \\(K=3\\pi\\). Proposition 4 gives a finitely supported real vector \\(h_1\\), the coefficients \\(h_j=0\\) for \\(j\\geq2\\), and a word \\(w\\in\\mathbb F_n\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|h_1\\|_{\\ell^2}&lt;\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\|D_w-\\delta_e\\|_{\\ell^2}&lt;\\eta.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 3 and put \\(\\beta=\\beta_1\\). Its estimates give\n\\[\n\\max_j\\|\\beta(A_j)-A_j\\|&lt;\\epsilon,\n\\qquad\n\\|\\beta(A_w)-CA_w\\|&lt;\\epsilon.\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(\\beta\\) fixes \\(S=\\arg(C)\\), and hence fixes \\(C=e^{iS}\\).</p><p>The free-group basis transformation fixing the first \\(n\\) symbols and sending the last symbol \\(c\\) to \\(cw\\) is invertible: its inverse fixes the first \\(n\\) symbols and sends \\(c\\) to \\(cw^{-1}\\). Proposition 1 therefore gives a trace-preserving normal automorphism \\(\\gamma\\) of \\(M\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\gamma(A_j)=A_j\\quad(1\\leq j\\leq n),\n\\qquad\n\\gamma(C)=CA_w.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\gamma^{-1}\\circ\\beta.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\gamma\\) fixes every \\(A_j\\), and hence \\(A_w\\),\n\\[\n\\alpha(A_j)-A_j\n=\\gamma^{-1}\\bigl(\\beta(A_j)-A_j\\bigr)\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\alpha(A_w)-C\n=\\gamma^{-1}\\bigl(\\beta(A_w)-CA_w\\bigr).\n\\]\nAn automorphism is isometric, so these identities prove the estimates, with the displayed factor order.</p><p>The image under \\(\\alpha\\) of the original freely generating Haar tuple is again such a tuple. Finally,\n\\[\nC'=\\alpha(C)=\\gamma^{-1}(C)=CA_w^*,\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\beta(C)=C\\) and\n\\[\n\\gamma(CA_w^*)=CA_wA_w^*=C.\n\\]</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 3, "cite"], [1, 3, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 6, "cite"], [4, 8, "cite"], [4, 9, "cite"], [4, 16, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [5, 10, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 5, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 0, "cite"]]});
